WEBVTT

00:00:02.938 --> 00:00:04.345
This is eodia insights:

00:00:04.345 --> 00:00:08.077
the open-source BI tool where
every query goes through Trino,

00:00:08.077 --> 00:00:11.258
and everyone sees only what
they are allowed to see.

00:00:12.686 --> 00:00:17.251
Meet Maison Arvor, an online shop:
three years of orders in PostgreSQL,

00:00:17.251 --> 00:00:19.566
and its customer service in MongoDB.

00:00:20.366 --> 00:00:21.692
Right from the home page,

00:00:21.692 --> 00:00:25.406
automatic exploration reads your
tables and pulls out the key figures.

00:00:26.365 --> 00:00:31.086
PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQL Server,
Oracle, Snowflake or MongoDB:

00:00:31.086 --> 00:00:35.885
every connected database becomes
a Trino catalog, on the fly.

00:00:39.416 --> 00:00:45.656
A question is built with the mouse: a
table, a filter, a measure, a grouping.

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:53.630
The chart draws itself as you go,
and changes shape in one click.

00:00:55.237 --> 00:01:00.357
Another table? Join it — here the
customers, to see revenue by region.

00:01:06.902 --> 00:01:10.182
The question is saved in a
folder, for the whole team.

00:01:14.184 --> 00:01:19.624
Calendars, market shares, bar
races: every story finds its chart.

00:01:23.481 --> 00:01:29.801
And a line extends in one click: the trend,
the season, and a margin of uncertainty.

00:01:32.783 --> 00:01:37.857
For those who prefer it, the SQL editor
speaks a single language, Trino's,

00:01:37.857 --> 00:01:39.023
for every source.

00:01:40.319 --> 00:01:45.599
MongoDB tickets join PostgreSQL customers,
like two tables of the same database.

00:01:53.414 --> 00:01:56.134
Or simply ask, in plain language.

00:01:58.832 --> 00:02:03.859
The assistant searches the schema, writes
the SQL, runs it under your permissions,

00:02:03.859 --> 00:02:05.392
and answers with a chart.

00:02:06.192 --> 00:02:10.192
The answer is kept as a
question, ready for a dashboard.

00:02:10.984 --> 00:02:14.584
Dashboards bring questions
together, always up to date.

00:02:15.384 --> 00:02:20.504
Filters drive the whole page, and a
click on a chart filters all the others.

00:02:23.023 --> 00:02:27.903
Tabs, automatic refresh, and
full screen for the meeting room.

00:02:31.881 --> 00:02:36.338
A dashboard is shared with a person
or a group, through a public link,

00:02:36.338 --> 00:02:38.121
or embedded in another site.

00:02:40.176 --> 00:02:45.104
A theme set on a folder dresses
everything in it: fonts, colors, logo,

00:02:45.104 --> 00:02:47.216
all the way to the PDF export.

00:02:53.283 --> 00:02:59.410
But the real difference is permissions.
They are set per group: a source, a table,

00:02:59.410 --> 00:03:00.083
a column.

00:03:01.254 --> 00:03:05.841
A row rule relies on the person's
attributes, here their region.

00:03:05.841 --> 00:03:08.134
And customer e-mails are masked.

00:03:09.448 --> 00:03:15.441
Before every query, Trino itself asks
eodia insights what the person may read.

00:03:15.441 --> 00:03:17.208
No path gets around it.

00:03:18.684 --> 00:03:24.364
Camille, an analyst in Brittany, opens the
same dashboard: only her data shows up.

00:03:25.909 --> 00:03:31.189
Even in free SQL, the other regions
don't exist, and e-mails stay masked.

00:03:34.013 --> 00:03:38.363
An instance splits into spaces,
one per team or subsidiary;

00:03:38.363 --> 00:03:41.533
a source can be shared
from one to another.

00:03:47.669 --> 00:03:51.783
And for your programs and agents,
a documented API and an MCP server,

00:03:51.783 --> 00:03:53.989
always under the token's permissions.

00:03:56.332 --> 00:04:02.391
Open source under the AGPL, eodia insights
installs in one command with Docker.

00:04:02.391 --> 00:04:03.772
Try the live demo.
